opic"]},{"id":"narrative","score":0.44,"confidence":"medium","evidence":["catalog text contains \"story of\"","catalog text contains \"story of how\""]},{"id":"synthesis","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"from\"","catalog text contains \"history of\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":false,"statement":"","confidence":0},"academicOrientation":{"score":45,"confidence":0.45},"profileConfidence":0.66,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"argument":{"statement":"Decisive-sounding battles usually do not determine wars’ outcomes directly; instead, battle outcomes often matter by accelerating the enemy’s defensive erosion within broader conflicts dominated by attrition and matériel.","confidence":0.52}},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"e01a4423d23b93970aea27c525bf47b1ba885d28a333a53c5a2760410621340b","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"summary":"\"History has tended to measure war's winners and losers in terms of its major engagements, battles in which the result was so clear-cut that they could be considered \"decisive.\" Cannae, Konigsberg, Austerlitz, Midway, Agincourt-all resonate in the literature of war and in our imaginations as tide-turning. But these legendary battles may or may not have determined the final outcome of the wars in which they were fought. Nor has the \"genius\" of the so-called Great Captains--from Alexander the Great to Frederick the Great and Napoleon--played a major role. Wars are decided in other ways. Cathal J. Nolan's The Allure of Battle systematically and engrossingly examines the great battles, tracing what he calls \"short-war thinking,\" the hope that victory might be swift and wars brief. As he proves persuasively, however, such has almost never been the case. Even the major engagements have mainly contributed to victory or defeat by accelerating the erosion of the other side's defences. Massive conflicts, the so-called \"people's wars,\" beginning with Napoleon and continuing until 1945, have consisted of and been determined by prolonged stalemate and attrition, industrial wars in which the determining factor has been not military but matériel. Nolan's masterful book places battles squarely and mercilessly within the context of the wider conflict in which they took place.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Military history","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-allure-of-battle-a-history-of-how-wars-have-been-won-and-lost-cathal-nolan"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Battles","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-allure-of-battle-a-history-of-how-wars-have-been-won-and-lost-cathal-nolan"}],"displaySummary":"As he proves persuasively, however, such has almost never been the case. Even the major engagements have mainly contributed to victory or defeat by accelerating the erosion of the other side's defences. Massive conflicts, the so-called \"people's wars,\" beginning with Napoleon and continuing until 1945, have consisted of and been determined by prolonged stalemate and attrition, industrial wars in which the determining factor has been not military but matériel. Nolan's masterful book places battles squarely and mercilessly within the context of the wider conflict in which they took place.","primarySubject":"War & Military","primaryTopic":"Energy, Extraction & Resources","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"War & Military","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"War & Military","score":6,"evidenceCount":1},{"subject":"History","score":3,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"Military history","mappedSubject":"War & Military","score":6,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-allure-of-battle-a-hist